{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.YoungDiagramCorner","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.IntervalCases","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.threeRow_vanishing_of_pinning","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.threeRow_vanishing_of_pinning","kind":"theorem","line":271,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L271-L306","doc":"**The composed statement: one scalar in, vanishing at every level out.**\n\n`threeRow_vanishing_all_levels` still asks for a base case quantified over\nshapes. `threeRow_size_three_subsingleton` collapses that quantifier, so the\ninput becomes a **single scalar fact** — the weight of the one three-row shape\nof size 3 is zero — which is exactly what\n`TemperleyLiebTower.conventionD_kills_antisymmetriser` delivers.\n\nThat is as far as this layer can honestly go, and the boundary is worth naming.\nGoing further means saying that the framework's `y_λ` *is* the Wedderburn weight\nof the Markov trace, i.e. `tr_M = Σ_λ y_λ χ_λ`. That is a statement about\ncharacters and the Wedderburn decomposition — the representation theory this\nmodule deliberately does not carry, and which the Hecke-instance layer\n(`QOU.Hecke`, PR #4857) is building. Until it lands, `y` here is any function\nsatisfying the hypotheses, and the theorem is a genuine implication rather than\na tautology: nothing below *defines* `y` to be zero anywhere.\n\nThe one place a reader could be misled is `hpin`. It is an input, not a\nconclusion. What is proved is that a single zero at `n = 3` propagates to every\nlevel — not that the zero is there. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.threeRow_vanishing_all_levels","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.threeRow_vanishing_all_levels","kind":"theorem","line":241,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L241-L269","doc":"**The propagation, with every combinatorial hypothesis discharged.**\n\nIf the weights are nonnegative, each cover is dominated by its parent, and all\n`≥ 3`-row weights vanish at three strands, then they vanish at every level\nabove. Nothing here is assumed about Young diagrams — `youngPred` supplies it.\n\nThe two remaining hypotheses are the *analytic* ones and both are proved\nelsewhere on this branch for the actual Markov weights: `hnn` from\n`TemperleyLiebTower.yw_nonneg`, and `hdom` from\n`TemperleyLiebTower.dominates_of_branching` applied to `yw_branching`. The base\ncase is `conventionD_kills_antisymmetriser`.\n\nSo the `≥ 3`-row vanishing at all `n ≥ 3` now rests on the `n = 3` pinning plus\nproved facts, with no appeal to Wenzl's theorem. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.threeRow_size_three_subsingleton","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.threeRow_size_three_subsingleton","kind":"theorem","line":230,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L230-L239","doc":"**Hence the base case needs only one scalar fact.** Any two size-3 shapes\nwith three rows are equal, so `∀ m, 3 ≤ rows m → y m = 0` collapses to the\nsingle instance the pinning already provides. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.card_three_threeRow_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.card_three_threeRow_unique","kind":"theorem","line":207,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L207-L228","doc":"**At three cells there is exactly one shape with three rows, namely `(1³)`.**\n\nThree rows forces `(0,0), (1,0), (2,0) ∈ μ` — that is already three cells — and\n`card = 3` leaves no room for a fourth. So the base case of the induction is a\nstatement about a *single* diagram, which is what lets the scalar pinning\n`TemperleyLiebTower.conventionD_kills_antisymmetriser` discharge it: there is\nnothing to quantify over.\n\nThis is the glue between the scalar layer (which knows `y₍₁³₎ = 0`) and the\ndiagram layer (which needs `∀ m of size 3 with ≥ 3 rows, y m = 0`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.youngPred","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.youngPred","kind":"theorem","line":186,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L186-L205","doc":"**The predecessor fact, on Young diagrams of a given size.**\n\nEvery shape of size `n+1` with at least three rows covers a shape of size `n`\nwith at least three rows, provided `3 ≤ n`. This is `hpred` of\n`vanishing_all_levels_dom`, discharged.\n\nThe `3 ≤ n` guard is exactly the `4 ≤ card` of `exists_threeRow_corner`, and it\nis load-bearing: at `n = 2` the shape `(1³)` of size 3 covers only `(1²)`, which\nhas two rows. That is the base case of the induction, not a step. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.exists_threeRow_corner","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.exists_threeRow_corner","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L111-L165","doc":"**THE THEOREM. A diagram with at least three rows and at least four cells has\na removable corner whose removal still leaves at least three rows.**\n\nThis is `hpred` of `QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.vanishing_step`, no longer a\nhypothesis. Three cases, each exhibiting an explicit corner:\n\n1. the last-row corner is not `(2,0)` — use it;\n2. it *is* `(2,0)`, and row 1 is shorter than row 0 — use the row-0 corner;\n3. it is `(2,0)` and rows 0 and 1 are equal — then they have length `≥ 2` (else\n   `μ = (1,1,1)`, ruled out by the cardinality hypothesis) and the row-1 corner\n   serves. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.card_le_three_of_rowLen_zero_le_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.card_le_three_of_rowLen_zero_le_one","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L92-L109","doc":"**A diagram with three rows whose first row is a single cell is `(1,1,1)`**,\nhence has three cells. This is the sole obstruction the cardinality hypothesis\nrules out. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.isCorner_lastRow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.isCorner_lastRow","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L82-L90","doc":"**The last cell of the last row is always a removable corner** of a nonempty\ndiagram — in closed form, needing no search. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.rowLen_eq_zero_of_colLen_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.rowLen_eq_zero_of_colLen_le","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L73-L80","doc":"Row `i` is empty once the rows run out. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.rowLen_pos_of_lt_colLen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.rowLen_pos_of_lt_colLen","kind":"theorem","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L67-L71","doc":"Row `i` is nonempty exactly when the diagram has more than `i` rows. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.isCorner_rowEnd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.isCorner_rowEnd","kind":"theorem","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L60-L65","doc":"**The last cell of a row is a removable corner exactly when the next row is\nstrictly shorter.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.threeRows_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.threeRows_iff","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/YoungDiagramThreeRowCorner.lean#L54-L58","doc":"**\"At least three rows\" is membership of `(2,0)`.** The row count of a Young\ndiagram is the length of column `0`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}